= Methods For NeuroSymbolic Reinforcement Learning <sec:methods>

\
\
This chapter addresses RQ1: how are established RL methods and components transformed into neurosymbolic counterparts? Following @sec:taxonomies, methods are grouped by the part of the RL agent that the symbolic component changes. @fig:rl-loop in @sec:background shows where each of these parts sits in the RL loop. For every family, the chapter describes the mechanism, the representative systems, what is learned and what must be supplied by hand, and the main limitations. @tab:families at the end of the chapter summarises the families and records the class each belongs to in the taxonomy of Yu et al. @yu2023.

== Symbolic state representation 

The most direct change to a deep RL agent is to replace its input. Instead of learning from pixels or flat feature vectors, the agent learns from a symbolic description of the state: a set of objects, their attributes and the relations between them. The policy can then be any RL method, from tabular Q-learning to a neural network, but it operates over symbols rather than raw perception.


This idea  actually predates deep learning. As mentioned above Relational RL (RRL) learns value functions over states described as logical facts, such as `On(blockA, blockB)`, and generalises across problems with different numbers of objects @dzeroski2001. Garnelo et al. revived it for deep RL with deep symbolic RL (DSRL), in which a neural network maps raw images to a symbolic representation of objects and their interactions, and a symbolic learner chooses actions over that representation @garnelo2016. Their system was a proof of concept on a simple game, but it set out the argument that has driven tge field since that of  symbolic representations should transfer better and be easier to inspect than learned features. 


Recent work concentrates on making the symbolic layer reliable and inspectable. OCAtari provides object-centric states for Atari games, which made symbolic agents testable on a standard benchmark @delfosse2024oc. SCoBots adds a concept bottleneck: objects are extracted first, relational concepts such as distances between objects are computed from them, and an interpretable policy acts on those concepts @delfosse2024. Because each each layer can be examined, a designer can find the concepts a policy depends on and remove ones that cause unintended behaviour.


_Learned versus given_ :The policy is learned; the vocabulary of objects and relations is designed by hand, and in most benchmarks the objects themselves are supplied by the environment rather than detected.


Limitation: The method moves the difficulty rather than removing it: the agent is only as good as the symbols it receives, which is the grounding problem of @sec:bg-grounding. 

== Logic policies

The second family changes the policy itself. Instead of a neural network, the policy is a set of logic rules whose heads are actions and whose bodies are conditions on the symbolic state. Because classical rules cannot be trained by gradient descent, these methods use differentiable logic (@sec:bg-learning): each candidate rule receives a weight, and the weights are learned from return.


Neural Logic Reinforcement Learning (NLRL) was the first to train such a policy with policy gradients, using the differentiable inductive logic programming framework of Evans and Grefenstette @jiang2019 @evans2018. NUDGE addresses the search problem differently. A pretrained neural agent acts as a guide, ranking candidate rules by how well they agree with its behaviour, and the weights of the selected rules are then trained with a logic actor and a neural critic @delfosse2023. The result is a policy that can be read directly. A rule learned in one of its environments, `jump(X) :- closeby(O1, O2), type(O1, agent), type(O2, enemy)`, states that the agent jumps when an enemy is close. On modified versions of its environments, NUDGE agents also outperformed neural PPO agents trained on the originals, for example scoring 3.60 against $-20.88$ on a harder variant of GetOut @delfosse2023.

Two other interesting systems relax the dependence on a pretrained neural policy. INSIGHT learns a compact symbolic policy together with the perception module that produces its inputs, and uses a language model to turn the learned policy into textual explanations @luo2024. BlendRL keeps both kinds of policy, a neural policy over pixels and a logic policy over objects are combined by a learned blending function, $pi = beta dot pi_"neural" + (1 - beta) dot pi_"logic"$, so that the agent can rely on logic for reasoning and on the neural policy for fine control @shindo2025. Its logic rules are generated by a language model rather than written by hand. On modified versions of three Atari games where a neural PPO agent scored zero, BlendRL continued to score well @shindo2025.



_Learned versus given._ : Rule weights are learned; rule templates, predicates and (in NUDGE) a pretrained neural policy are supplied.


Limitation: Expressiveness is bounded by the candidate rules, and the readability of the policy depends on the number of rules with non-negligible weight.

== Programmatic policies

A third family represents the policy as a program in a domain-specific language, with conditionals, loops and calls to perception and action primitives. Programs are more expressive than single rules and can often be verified with standard program analysis tools. The challenge is search, since the space of programs is discrete and very large.

Three strategies have been identified. _Imitation of a neural oracle_ trains a neural policy first and then searches for a program that reproduces its behaviour. PIRL guides this search with the neural policy @verma2018, and VIPER uses imitation learning to extract a decision tree that can then be formally verified @bastani2018. _Search in a learned program space_ avoids the oracle: LEAPS learns a continuous embedding of programs and searches it directly for a program that maximises return @trivedi2021. _Generation by language models_ asks an LLM to write the program from a task description. Code as Policies uses this approach to produce executable robot control code @liang2023. Distillation is also used to deploy RL in practice: DeRAN distils deep RL policies for 5G network control into closed-form expressions and first-order rules, recovering 78% to 87% of the original performance while making every decision auditable @lu2026.


_Learned versus given_ : The program is learned or generated; the DSL is designed by hand. 


Limitation: When a program imitates a neural policy, any verification applies to the program, not to the neural policy it approximates @bastani2018.

== Symbolic reward and task structure

The fourth family leaves the policy and state unchanged and instead gives the reward function a symbolic form. This is the clearest example of reasoning for learning (described in the last chapter) the symbolic component does not act, but it shapes what the neural learner is trying to achieve.

Reward machines express the reward as a finite-state machine over high-level events (@sec:bg-automata) @toroicarte2022. Because the structure of the task is visible, the agent can exploit it in three ways: (1) automated reward shaping from the machine's states, (2 ) decompositionn of the task into subtasks, and finally (3) counterfactual learning, in which a single experience is used to update the value of every machine state at once. Tasks written in LTL and other formal languages can be compiled into reward machines automatically @camacho2019, and when the machine is not known in advance it can be learned. Furelos-Blanco et al. interleave RL with ILASP, inducing a subgoal automaton from the traces the agent generates and refining it when it fails to explain a new trace @furelosblanco2021.


The same principle has been applied outside classical RL. RLSF fine-tunes language models with RL using rewards computed by symbolic tools such as compilers and solvers, which return certificates that pinpoint errors rather than a single score @jha2024.


_Learned versus given_.The policy is learned; the reward machine is usually given, though it can be learned or compiled. 

Limitation: A reward machine is only as good as its labelling function, which must report high-level events correctly, and a wrong machine gives a confidently wrong learning signal.

== Symbolic high-level control

The fifth family splits control into levels. Low-level skills, modelled as options (@sec:bg-rl), are learned by neural RL, and a symbolic component decides which skill to use. This mirrors the planning and reasoning layer of classical AI, with learned skills replacing hand-coded actions.

PEORL combines an answer set planner with hierarchical RL: the planner proposes a sequence of high-level actions, RL learns how to execute them, and the measured outcomes feed back into the planner to improve the plan @yang2018peorl. SDRL extends this into a planner, controller and meta-controller architecture: the planner uses symbolic knowledge for long-term planning, the controller learns skills with deep RL from intrinsic rewards, and the meta-controller evaluates the controller and proposes new goals @lyu2019. DUA takes the same structure but learns the high-level component instead of planning with a given model @mitchener2022. A detector produces symbolic facts, an answer set program chooses which of nine pre trained skills to run,and the program's weak constraints are learned with ILASP from one training arena per task category. On the Animal-AI testbed, it achieved state-of-the-art (at that time) results in the categories it trained on and would have placed third overall in the competition @mitchener2022 @crosby2019.


_Learned versus given_. Skills are learned; the high-level model is either given (PEORL, SDRL) or learned from few examples (DUA). 

Limitation:  The skills and the symbolic vocabulary must be designed in advance. DUA, for instance, fails every task involving objects its hand-crafted detector cannot see @mitchener2022.


== Symbolic constraints on actions

The sixth family restricts what the agent is allowed to do. The policy and reward are unchanged, but a symbolic component filters, corrects or reweights actions before they reach the environment.

_Shielding_ synthesises a shield from a formal safety specification and an abstract model of the environment; the shield removes unsafe actions or replaces them with safe ones, giving guarantees that hold as far as the model is accurate @alshiekh2018. Probabilistic logic shields extend this to uncertain perception: using ProbLog, they compute the probability that each action is safe given probabilistic observations, and reweight the policy towards safe actions in a way that remains differentiable, so that safety can be enforced during training as well as deployment @yang2023. Neurosymbolic action masking learns a mapping from states to symbolic models that encode domain constraints, and uses it to mask infeasible actions, reporting near-zero constraint violations on constrained puzzles such as Sudoku and N-Queens @han2026. Khandelwal et al. sates that constraints of this kind should live in an editable knowledge graph that the agent consults at execution time, so that a change in requirements becomes an edit rather than a retraining run @khandelwal2026.


_Learned versus given_ : The policy is learned; constraints are given as specifications or rules. 


Limitation:   Guarantees cover the symbolic constraint, not the neural perception that decides whether the constraint applies.

== Symbolic knowledge as a prior

The final family uses symbolic knowledge to start or steer learning, after which the neural agent may move away from it. PROLONETS encodes human-specified propositional rules directly as the initial structure and weights of a neural policy, giving the agent a warm start that RL then refines @silva2021. SR-DQN uses ASP rules learned on small instances of a task to bias exploration and rescale the Q-values of a DQN agent on larger instances, with the influence of the rules decaying over training @veronese2026. Its authors report better sample efficiency than reward machine baselines and robustness to imperfect rules.


_Learned versus given_. The final policy is learned; the prior is given or learned on simpler tasks. 


Limitation: Because the knowledge is gradually overridden, the final policy is no more interpretable than an ordinary neural one.


== Summary

@tab:families summarises the families. We make the following observations that answers RQ1.



First, we note symbolic components enter every part of the RL agent, but they rarely replace the neural components entirely. In most families the neural part still handles perception and low-level control, and the symbolic part supplies structure,a state vocabulary, a rule set, a task specification, a high-level plan or a constraint.



Second, the families differ sharply in what must be designed by hand. Every family needs a symbolic vocabulary, and most need more, like, rule templates, a DSL, a reward machine, a planning model or a safety specification. The trend in recent work is to learn or generate these components, through ILP @mitchener2022 @furelosblanco2021, differentiable logic @delfosse2023 or language models @shindo2025 @liang2023, rather than writing them.



Third, the families do not map cleanly onto the classes of Yu et al. Logic policies and symbolic state representations are mostly learning for reasoning, and reward structure, constraints and priors are mostly reasoning for learning, but hierarchical systems and blended policies fall between classes. This supports the decision in @sec:taxonomies to organise methods by RL component and treat the direction of benefit as a secondary property.

